  Basin Commission's are increasingly  active in regulating shale gas  development or at least trying to and  then local governments as Professor John  Nolan will discuss are also increasingly  involved although in  cases states are trying to preempt local  regulation but states are at the center  of all of this again this is in part  because of a few exemptions it's a  politically loaded term I don't intend  it that way it's simply that in the past  Congress has determined that a certain  aspects of federal statutes and EPA  implementation of the statutes through  regulation shouldn't apply to oil and  gas one of the big exemptions is Rick  raw resource conservation Recovery Act  which applies to waste the handling of  waste from cradle to grave subtitle C of  that act which applies to hazardous  waste does not address many exploration  and production wastes from oil and gas  so the states are primarily responsible  for those wastes we also have the actual  injection of water and chemicals down  the well that's a very small phase of a  much larger process but the injection of  watering chemicals down the well the  actual hydraulic fracturing is not  regulated by the safe drinking water act  although fracturing with diesel is and  the EPA has just developed guidelines  for fracturing with diesel we have some  very important federal laws that do  apply again the Safe Drinking Water Act  governs the safety of underground  injection control wells that except  produced waters and flow back waters  from many drilled and fractured wells we  also have new a Clean Air Act standards  coming in for the volatile organic  compounds that are emitted from the  flowback water by approximately january  first 2015 operators will essentially  have to capture those VOCs and send them  through pipeline rather than letting  them out into the air I mentioned the  diesel fuel fracturing standards the  Endangered Species Act a migratory bird  treaty Zack also very important a lot of  oil and gas attorneys spend their time  trying to figure out how to prevent  Golden Eagles from roosting on rigs as  well as keeping birds out of surface  pits there have been some interesting  prosecutions recently in the Bakken  Shale by the Fish and Wildlife Service  dead ducks were found in the surface  reserve pits  a federal court however said that there  was not a direct killing of the Ducks by  the oil and gas company in the Bakken so  the prosecution's were reversed but  Texas the federal courts in Texas  disagree with that determination storm  water permits we've talked a little bit  about sediment and erosion when an oil  or gas site is constructed the operator  must obtain a federal Clean Water Act  permit called a stormwater nip T's  permit typically state implemented and  then finally the EPA is developing clean  pretreatment standards for flow back and  other liquids waste sent from shale gas  wells to wastewater treatment plants but  the states are the most important and  let me just briefly go through some of  the major stages of well development and  give you a few examples from the state's  various states are attempted to  essentially regulate nuisances  associated with shot holes or the vibra  size operations a thumper buggy or some  other equipment hits the ground to  create the vibrations that we heard  about earlier today and essentially you  want to separate those operations from  houses in some cases from water water  wells this Colorado regulation is for a  shot hole you've basically to fill in a  shot hole if you dynamite the hole and  you have to rake around it basically  restore the site to its previous  condition Maryland might even deny a  vibro size permit or shot hole hole  permit if it could substantially damage  the environment and someone asked about  cutting of trees earlier Arkansas  actually prevents the cutting of trees  for vibra size or shot whole operations  trees that are six inches or more in  diameter in terms of building the